  [math chapter2] L30: Its main problem is one of circularity: to know what it is for a thing to be believed to be $F$, we must first know what $F$ is. A related problem is the infinite regress: if $[x \text{ is good}] = [I \text{ believe } x \text{ is good}]$, then $[I \text{ believe } x \text{ is good}] \approx [I \text{ believe } (I \text{ believe } x \text{ is good})]$, and so on. In general, it is incoherent to postulate a proposition whose truth would consist solely in your believing it. The same point applies to the view that to be good is to be believed to be good by society, or by God, or by anyone else, and the same applies if we substitute 'perceived', 'known', 'asserted', or any other verb for 'believed'. The word 'good' should not appear within the explanation of what it is for $x$ to be good.
  [math chapter2]   → Its main problem is one of circularity: to know what it is for a thing to be believed to be $F$, we must first know what $F$ is. A related problem is the infinite regress: if $\langle x \text{ is good}\rangle = \langle I \text{ believe } x \text{ is good}\rangle$, then $\langle I \text{ believe } x \text{ is good}\rangle \approx \langle I \text{ believe } (I \text{ believe } x \text{ is good})\rangle$, and so on. In general, it is incoherent to postulate a proposition whose truth would consist solely in your believing it. The same point applies to the view that to be good is to be believed to be good by society, or by God, or by anyone else, and the same applies if we substitute 'perceived', 'known', 'asserted', or any other verb for 'believed'. The word 'good' should not appear within the explanation of what it is for $x$ to be good.
